  "worked_solution": "Let $f(t)=3-\\sqrt{t-2}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing for $t\\ge 2.$ Note that we subtract $\\sqrt{t-2}$ to indicate the rate at which the volume is decreasing.\n\nLet $F(T)$ be the volume of the pool after $T$ minutes. Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T)=F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the pool contains $6+2\\cdot 3=12$ gallons of water. Substituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{\\int }_{2}^{T}(3-\\sqrt{t-2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{(3t-\\frac{2}{3}{(t-2)}^{3/2})|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =12+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}-(3(2)-\\frac{2}{3}(2-2)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =6+3T-\\frac{2}{3}(T-2)^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of our original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t)=6+3t-\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)^{3/2},\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
